<section id="conclusions">
  <div class="section-label">Section 06</div>
  <h2>Conclusions &amp; Limitations</h2>
  <p class="lead">The 11-year record reveals a broadly stable examination framework with two notable structural inflection points, a consistently dual-pillar content strategy, and recent signs of curriculum broadening that merit close monitoring.</p>

  <h3>Key Findings Summary</h3>
  <div class="insight-grid">
    <div class="insight-card">
      <div class="ic-title">Structural Stability with Defined Phases</div>
      <div class="ic-body">MCQs (always 15) and Long Questions (always 3) have never changed. The exam has passed through three short-question phases: 9 (2015–16), 18 (2017–19), 15 (2021–present). The current 33-question format is the most stable phase in the dataset.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="insight-card green">
      <div class="ic-title">Two Immovable Content Pillars</div>
      <div class="ic-body">C Programming and Database/DBMS collectively account for nearly all question content across every year. This dual focus has remained constant regardless of question count changes — it is the invariant core of BIEK Computer Science Paper-II.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="insight-card orange">
      <div class="ic-title">2025: Broadening Scope</div>
      <div class="ic-body">The 2025 paper introduced more new sub-topics than any prior year: binary search, ACID properties, bitwise operators, ER diagrams, storage classes, file handling, structures/unions. Whether this is a one-off or signals a curriculum update warrants ongoing monitoring.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="insight-card purple">
      <div class="ic-title">Exam Predictability is High</div>
