  </div>
  <div class="callout warning">
    <strong>3. Transcription accuracy.</strong> As a community-compiled dataset, minor transcription errors in the question column are possible. Topic frequency analysis is based on keyword matching in question text — typographical variants may cause slight undercounting of some topics.
  </div>
  <div class="callout warning">
    <strong>4. Statistical power.</strong> With only 327 rows across 10 years and many sub-topic categories appearing just once or twice, fine-grained frequency differences should be interpreted cautiously. Broad patterns (the dual C+Database pillar, the three structural phases) are robust; single-year emergence of new topics is a signal, not a confirmed trend.
  </div>

  <h3>Recommendations for Follow-Up Analysis</h3>
  <p>Future work that would meaningfully extend this analysis includes: (1) sourcing the 2020 paper to fill the dataset gap; (2) coding annual vs. supplementary paper labels to separate their question sets; (3) tracking whether 2025's new topics (ACID, ER diagrams, binary search, storage classes) recur in 2026; and (4) conducting a marks-weighting analysis if official mark schemes become available, since question counts alone do not reflect marks allocation within long questions.</p>
